---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BitTokenDiagram from '../components/BitTokenDiagram.astro';
import RoundsInspector from '../components/RoundsInspector.astro';
import { progress } from '../lib/data';
import { thousands } from '../lib/format';

const model = progress.current!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const share = (n: number) => `${((100 * n) / model.parameters).toFixed(1)}%`;
---

<Layout
  title="Model: a width-free bit-level transformer"
  description="How Teenie works: one token per answer bit, plus scratch registers and worked examples that let a small transformer compute through products, up to 32 bits."
  keywords={['bit-level transformer', 'scratch registers', 'model architecture', 'width-free model', 'parameter breakdown']}
  kind="article"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Model</p>
  <h1>The current model</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Nobody tells it which rule it faces, and its weights don't change while it answers. It reads the 999 examples,
    guesses every bit of the answer, then corrects that guess over several rounds using the same weights each time.
    Nothing in it is sized for a particular width, which is why one set of weights answers both 16- and 32-bit
    questions.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-3 stats">
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Parameters</span>
      <span class="value">{thousands(model.parameters)}</span>
      <span class="note">the same at every width up to {model.span} bits</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Bit layers · rounds</span>
      <span class="value">{model.layers} · {model.rounds}</span>
      <span class="note">{`width ${model.hidden}, ${model.heads} attention heads, the same weights every round`}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Scratch registers · worked examples</span>
      <span class="value">{model.registers} · {model.worked_examples}</span>
      <span class="note">room for intermediate values, and examples worked through like questions</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>How it answers</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <BitTokenDiagram layers={model.layers} rounds={model.rounds} heads={model.heads} />
  </div>
  <div class="grid-2 steps">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">1 · See every bit from where it sits</p>
      <p>
        Each bit of every example, and of the question, is a token that sees the numbers relative to its own position
        (31 bits either side) and also knows how far it sits from the lowest bit. No layer depends on the width, so the
        same weights read 8, 16 or 32 bits.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">2 · One token per answer bit</p>
      <p>
        The question holds one token per answer bit. Every round the answer bits attend to each other, which lets
        carries and partial products move between positions, and each one reads the same bit of all 999 examples.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">3 · Scratch registers and worked examples</p>
      <p>
        {`Besides its guess, every question keeps ${model.registers} scratch numbers it writes and reads back each round, and ${model.worked_examples} examples are worked through the same way, so the answer bits can read what was worked out for real examples next to their true answers.`}
        It was this change that first let the model compute through a product rather than match examples.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">4 · Answer, and say how sure</p>
      <p>
        After the last round each answer bit comes out as a probability. The answer takes the likelier value at each
        position, its confidence is that of its least certain bit, and an answer only counts as sure when every bit is
        at 0.99 or above.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Where the parameters are</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>part</th>
          <th>what it does</th>
          <th class="num">parameters</th>
          <th class="num">share</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          model.parts.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <b>{p.part}</b>
              </td>
              <td class="muted">{p.detail}</td>
              <td class="num">{thousands(p.parameters)}</td>
              <td class="num">{share(p.parameters)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
        <tr>
          <td><b>Total</b></td>
          <td></td>
          <td class="num"><b>{thousands(model.parameters)}</b></td>
          <td class="num">100%</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    {`Checkpoint ${model.checkpoint}, update ${model.update}; source src/teenie/bits/wide_model.py. Every design we tried on the way here, including the ones that failed, is in the `}<a
      href={`${base}/changelog/`}>changelog</a
    > and the <a href={`${base}/findings/`}>findings</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Watch an earlier model answer</h2>
  <p>
    A recording from the earlier 8-bit version of the same design, small enough to step through. The guess starts out
    undecided and sharpens each round as the answer bits look back at the examples. We don't judge the model on 8-bit
    results, because lookup alone does well at that width.
  </p>
  <RoundsInspector />
</Layout>

<style>
  .stats {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .steps {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .steps p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
